# ESP32 L2 Wi-Fi Sniffer & Frame Analyzer



A lightweight, zero-copy IEEE 802.11 packet sniffer and wireless intrusion detection monitor built for the ESP32 microcontroller using ESP-IDF and FreeRTOS.



## Overview



This project passively captures 802.11 management and data frames in promiscuous mode and performs real-time L2 header analysis. It isolates raw frame capture from frame parsing across CPU cores to prevent buffer overflows and ensure zero packet loss during peak traffic bursts.



## Key Architectural Highlights



- **Decoupled Core Pipelining:** 

  - **Core 0:** Hardware Wi-Fi RX interrupt handler (ISR) and non-blocking packet ingestion into a dedicated FreeRTOS ring buffer.

  - **Core 1:** Asynchronous payload parsing, state tracking, and deauthentication frame anomaly detection.

- **Zero-Allocation Runtime:** Fixed-size ring buffer (`xRingbuffer`) allocated at initialization with zero dynamic memory allocations (`malloc`/`free`) during runtime packet reception.

- **Framework:** Native ESP-IDF v5.x via PlatformIO.



## Project Structure



```text

├── src/

│   ├── main.c                     # System initialization and core-pinned task setup

│   ├── net/

│   │   ├── wifi_sniffer.h         # Promiscuous driver API

│   │   ├── wifi_sniffer.c         # ISR handler & ring buffer producer

│   │   └── ieee80211_types.h      # Packed 802.11 frame definitions

│   └── processing/

│       ├── frame_analyzer.h       # Analysis task entry point

│       └── frame_analyzer.c       # Frame consumer & L2 parser

├── platformio.ini                 # Build targets and compiler flags

├── LICENSE                        # MIT License

└── README.md

```



## Getting Started



### Prerequisites



- [Visual Studio Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/)

- [PlatformIO IDE](https://platformio.org/) extension

- ESP32 Development Board (e.g., ESP32-WROOM-32)



### Build & Flash



1. Clone this repository:


```bash
git clone https://github.com/kris22871/esp32-l2-frame-analyzer.git
cd esp32-l2-frame-analyzer
```



2. Build the firmware:


```bash
pio run
```



3. Flash the firmware and open the serial monitor:


```bash
pio run -t upload -t monitor
```



